B.MQ=s(["Retained awareness and responsiveness","May consist of motor, sensory, autonomic or purely psychic (aura) symptoms",'Often the "warning" phase preceding a focal impaired-awareness seizure'],t.s)
B.Uy=new A.cP("Focal aware seizure","Focal","A focal seizure in which awareness is fully preserved throughout the event.",B.MQ,"Focal interictal spikes/sharp waves over the corresponding cortex; the ictal discharge is often subtle on scalp EEG.","A focal aware seizure is frequently just the aura preceding a focal impaired-awareness seizure.")
B.Me=s(["Behavioral arrest with impaired responsiveness","Oroalimentary and/or manual automatisms","Postictal confusion","May secondarily generalize to a bilateral tonic-clonic seizure"],t.s)
B.Uz=new A.cP("Focal impaired awareness seizure","Focal","A focal seizure with altered awareness or responsiveness, classically of temporal lobe onset, often with automatisms.",B.Me,"Focal (most often temporal) rhythmic ictal discharge; interictal temporal spikes.","Automatisms occurring with preserved but impaired responsiveness is the defining feature.")
B.LU=s(["Tonic stiffening then clonic jerking","Cyanosis and lateral tongue biting are common","Urinary incontinence may occur","Prolonged postictal confusion and sleepiness"],t.s)
B.UD=new A.cP("Generalized tonic-clonic seizure","Generalized","Bilateral tonic stiffening followed by clonic jerking with loss of consciousness from onset, or secondarily generalized from a focal seizure.",B.LU,"Generalized high-amplitude polyspike or spike-wave discharge; marked postictal generalized voltage attenuation.","Lateral tongue bite and prolonged postictal confusion are the two most useful bedside discriminators from PNES.")
B.Ns=s(["Typically lasts under 10-20 seconds","No aura and no postictal state","Can be provoked by 3 minutes of hyperventilation in clinic","Usual onset in childhood"],t.s)
B.UB=new A.cP("Absence seizure","Generalized","Brief, abrupt-onset and abrupt-offset staring spells with impaired awareness and no postictal confusion.",B.Ns,"Classic generalized 3 Hz spike-and-wave discharge.","Hyperventilation testing in clinic can reliably provoke a typical absence seizure.")
B.Mc=s(["Very brief (under 1 second) jerks","Often clustered shortly after awakening","Seen in juvenile myoclonic epilepsy and the progressive myoclonic epilepsies"],t.s)
B.Ur=new A.cP("Myoclonic seizure","Generalized","Sudden, brief, shock-like jerks, usually bilateral, without loss of consciousness if isolated.",B.Mc,"Generalized polyspike-and-wave discharge time-locked to the jerk.","Ask specifically about morning jerks causing dropped objects \u2014 a classic clue to juvenile myoclonic epilepsy.")
B.NJ=s(["Sudden, brief loss of tone","High risk of head/facial injury from falls","Common in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome"],t.s)
B.UI=new A.cP("Atonic seizure","Generalized",'Sudden loss of muscle tone causing a head nod or full collapse ("drop attack").',B.NJ,"Generalized slow spike-and-wave, or a generalized electrodecremental (voltage attenuation) pattern.","Consider a protective helmet for patients with frequent atonic drop attacks.")
B.NK=s(["Sustained, non-rhythmic stiffening","Frequently arises from sleep","Common in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome"],t.s)
B.Uw=new A.cP("Tonic seizure","Generalized","Sustained stiffening of the limbs and/or trunk lasting seconds, often nocturnal.",B.NK,"Generalized low-voltage fast activity or an electrodecremental response.",null)
B.Nr=s(["Rhythmic, repetitive jerking","No preceding tonic stiffening","Most common in the neonatal/infant period"],t.s)
B.Us=new A.cP("Clonic seizure","Generalized","Rhythmic bilateral jerking without a preceding tonic phase; most common in neonates and young infants.",B.Nr,"Generalized rhythmic spike or polyspike activity.",null)
B.Mi=s(["Mirthless, stereotyped laughter","Awareness may be preserved or impaired","Strongly associated with hypothalamic hamartoma; can also arise from temporal/frontal lesions"],t.s)
B.Uv=new A.cP("Gelastic seizure","Focal (hypothalamic)","Stereotyped, mirthless laughter without genuine mirth, classically caused by a hypothalamic hamartoma.",B.Mi,"Often unremarkable on scalp EEG given the deep hypothalamic origin; intracranial recording may be needed.","Gelastic seizures with precocious puberty in a child should prompt MRI for a hypothalamic hamartoma.")
B.N1=s(["Stereotyped, mirthless crying","Also associated with hypothalamic hamartoma"],t.s)
B.UG=new A.cP("Dacrystic seizure","Focal (hypothalamic)","The rarer counterpart of gelastic seizures, with stereotyped crying rather than laughing.",B.N1,"Similar to gelastic seizures \u2014 often unremarkable on scalp EEG.",null)
B.NY=s(["Perimenstrual (C1), periovulatory (C2), or luteal-inadequate (C3) patterns","Diagnosed with a seizure diary correlated to a menstrual calendar","May respond to adjunctive cyclical progesterone or acetazolamide"],t.s)
B.Uu=new A.cP("Catamenial epilepsy","Epilepsy pattern","Seizures that cluster in relation to the menstrual cycle, driven by cyclical estrogen (proconvulsant) and progesterone (anticonvulsant) fluctuation.",B.NY,"Reflects the patient's underlying focal or generalized epilepsy.","A seizure diary kept alongside a menstrual calendar is the key diagnostic tool.")
B.ML=s(["Stereotyped trigger-to-seizure relationship","Includes photosensitive, reading, musicogenic, hot water and startle epilepsy","Trigger avoidance is a first-line management strategy"],t.s)
B.UA=new A.cP("Reflex epilepsy","Reflex epilepsy","An umbrella term for seizures consistently triggered by a specific sensory stimulus or activity.",B.ML,"Trigger-specific findings, e.g. a photoparoxysmal response to intermittent photic stimulation.",null)
B.Mp=s(["Photoparoxysmal response on intermittent photic stimulation","Often generalized tonic-clonic or myoclonic seizures","More common in children and adolescents with genetic generalized epilepsy"],t.s)
B.UJ=new A.cP("Photosensitive epilepsy","Reflex epilepsy","Seizures triggered by flickering light or high-contrast patterns (strobe lights, screens, video games).",B.Mp,"Photoparoxysmal response to intermittent photic stimulation.","Polarizing sunglasses or covering one eye can help abort an ongoing photic-triggered seizure.")
B.N4=s(["Perioral/jaw myoclonus while reading","May generalize if the trigger is not stopped","Usually a benign, genetically determined reflex epilepsy"],t.s)
B.UL=new A.cP("Reading epilepsy","Reflex epilepsy","Jaw or perioral myoclonus triggered specifically by reading, especially aloud, which can progress to a generalized seizure if reading continues.",B.N4,"Focal (perisylvian/language network) discharges triggered by reading.",null)
B.MY=s(["Trigger is highly individual-specific music","Usually temporal lobe (often right-sided) semiology","Very rare"],t.s)
B.UH=new A.cP("Musicogenic epilepsy","Reflex epilepsy","A rare reflex epilepsy in which specific music triggers a seizure, typically of temporal lobe onset.",B.MY,"Temporal discharges elicited during the triggering stimulus.",null)
B.NT=s(["Triggered specifically by hot water exposure, especially over the head","Often focal seizures with impaired awareness"],t.s)
B.UK=new A.cP("Hot water epilepsy","Reflex epilepsy","Seizures triggered by contact with hot water, classically while bathing; more prevalent in some geographic populations.",B.NT,"Focal temporal discharge.",null)
B.MT=s(["Triggered by an unexpected startle","Often associated with an underlying structural brain abnormality or perinatal injury","Asymmetric tonic posturing is typical"],t.s)
B.UE=new A.cP("Startle epilepsy","Reflex epilepsy","Seizures triggered by a sudden, unexpected stimulus (loud noise, tap), typically producing an asymmetric tonic seizure.",B.MT,"Rapid generalized or bilateral discharge time-locked to the startle stimulus.",null)
B.Mg=s(["Onset typically 3-12 months of age","Clustered brief spasms (flexion, extension, or mixed)","Developmental regression is a red flag","Requires urgent evaluation \u2014 ACTH/vigabatrin are first-line"],t.s)
B.Ux=new A.cP("Infantile spasms (West syndrome)","Epilepsy syndrome","Clusters of brief flexor, extensor, or mixed spasms in infancy, part of West syndrome (spasms, hypsarrhythmia, developmental regression).",B.Mg,"Hypsarrhythmia: chaotic, high-amplitude slow activity with multifocal spikes.","Infantile spasms are a pediatric neurologic emergency \u2014 early treatment improves developmental outcome.")
B.L7=s(["Early-morning myoclonic jerks, often shortly after awakening","Generalized tonic-clonic seizures are common","Sleep deprivation and alcohol are classic triggers","Lifelong treatment is usually required \u2014 high relapse rate off medication"],t.s)
B.Ut=new A.cP("Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy","Epilepsy syndrome","The most common genetic generalized epilepsy syndrome, presenting in adolescence with morning myoclonic jerks.",B.L7,"Generalized polyspike-and-wave, often with a photosensitive response.",'Ask every adolescent with "clumsiness" or morning jerks about JME before labeling episodes non-epileptic.')
B.LV=s(["Forced/resisted eye closure","Asynchronous limb thrashing, side-to-side head shaking","Waxing-and-waning course; often prolonged (over 2 minutes)","Pelvic thrusting","Preserved awareness during bilateral limb movement is a specific sign"],t.s)
B.UF=new A.cP("Psychogenic Non-Epileptic Seizure (PNES)","Non-epileptic","Episodes resembling seizures caused by psychological factors rather than abnormal cortical electrical activity; confirmed by video-EEG capturing a typical event without an ictal correlate.",B.LV,"No ictal epileptiform correlate on video-EEG during a typical captured event.","PNES and epilepsy commonly coexist \u2014 a PNES diagnosis does not exclude comorbid epilepsy.")
B.NW=s(["Presyncopal prodrome: lightheadedness, tunnel vision, diaphoresis, nausea","Brief duration of jerking, usually under 30 seconds","Rapid, full recovery without prolonged postictal confusion","Situational triggers: prolonged standing, pain, venipuncture, micturition/defecation"],t.s)
B.UC=new A.cP("Convulsive syncope","Non-epileptic","Transient loss of consciousness from cerebral hypoperfusion accompanied by brief myoclonic jerks or tonic posturing \u2014 a common seizure mimic.",B.NW,"Generalized slowing then flattening during hypoperfusion, normalizing rapidly afterward \u2014 no epileptiform correlate.","Convulsive syncope is the single most common seizure mimic misdiagnosed as epilepsy in adults.")
B.li=s([B.Uy,B.Uz,B.UD,B.UB,B.Ur,B.UI,B.Uw,B.Us,B.Uv,B.UG,B.Uu,B.UA,B.UJ,B.UL,B.UH,B.UK,B.UE,B.Ux,B.Ut,B.UF,B.UC],A.ah("y<cP>"))
